  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 155 PM CDT Wed Sep 2 2026\n\n...Synopsis...\n\nA few isolated sprinkles near the South Dakota border this\nmorning as a weak embedded shortwave moved though, but fairly\ntranquil now with surface high pressure and the instability\npushed south of our area. Instability will return tonight into\ntomorrow as surface winds shift to the east southeast, and a\nlead shortwave coming out from the western Plains into Manitoba\nwill provide the focus for some convection tomorrow. Blocky \nupper ridging will build into the region for Friday and continue\ninto the weekend, with some ridge riding shortwaves coming \nthrough. The fairly active pattern will continue into Monday and\nTuesday as the upper ridge shifts slightly east and \nsouthwesterly flow aloft sets up over our area. Some of the \ndeterministic solutions try to break down the ridge just to our \neast and shift flow to a more west to northwesterly direction, \nwith cluster analysis showing about 30 percent of members \nagreeing while the majority of the ensemble keeps us in ridging \nor southwesterly flow.\n\n...Severe chances Thursday...\n\nBoth the high resolution and synoptic models are all over the\nplace as to how far north the highest instability gets tomorrow\nand where and when they break out convection. A few like the NAM\nkeep most of the surface based CAPE south of the SD border,\nwhile the GFS brings a narrow band of instability all the way up\nto Highway 200. Much will depend on what happens tonight, as\nseveral of the CAMs bring out some storms into the southern Red\nRiver Valley tomorrow morning with a weak lead shortwave. Some\nof the CAMs intensify the early activity fairly quickly\ntomorrow, with updraft helicity tracks starting to show up\nbefore 18Z, while other models don't have anything until the\nmain shortwave comes out later in the afternoon and evening.\nEven with the morning convection, several solutions show enough\nrecovery time to get over 2000 J/kg ML CAPE again in the\nafternoon, and with 40 kts of deep layer shear, severe weather\nis possible and the slight risk has been expanded northward.\nStorm mode is also not well resolved, with several CAMs showing\nlinear storms along the surface boundary while others like the\nHRRR have a much more curved hodograph and supercells. At this\npoint, all severe impacts are on the table, and will see how\nthings develop during the day tomorrow. \n \n...Heat Friday and into the weekend...\n\nWith rising heights as the upper ridge builds Friday and into\nthe weekend, temperatures will be well above average into the\nweekend. The ECMWF EFI shows a strong signal for warm temps,\nparticularly in the southern Red River Valley. Apparent temps do\nnot get above advisory criteria unless looking at the max\npercentile members, but the wet bulb globe gets into the 80s and\nheat risk is up into the major category for portions of the\nsouthern Red River Valley and west central MN both Friday and \nSaturday. May not need headlines especially this far in advance,\nbut will continue to message summer temps heading into the\nweekend.",
